          "email": "green@whamcloud.com",
          "date": "2026-09-06 05:33:32.000000000",
          "tz": -240
        },
        "subject": "LU-20698 osd: lock the os_has_ml_file scrub bit",
        "message": "LU-20698 osd: lock the os_has_ml_file scrub bit\n\nosd_iit_iget() sets os_has_ml_file without ->os_lock, and it runs on\nthe otable iterator thread as well as the OI scrub thread.  That bit\nshares a byte with os_full_scrub, which osd_scrub_oi_insert() sets\nfrom another thread under ->os_lock; both compile to a plain byte\nread-modify-write (orb $0x2 / orb $0x1 on the same address), so\neither update can be lost.  A dropped os_has_ml_file skips\nosd_scan_ml_file_main(), leaving multiply-linked OST objects\nunhandled for that pass; a dropped os_full_scrub skips the escalation\nto a full scrub.\n\nTake ->os_lock, as lustre_scrub.h already requires for every bit in\nthat word.  The osd-zfs store cannot lose an update today, because it\nand the only other write to its byte -- scrub_thread_prep() clearing\nos_full_scrub -- both run on the scrub thread.  That is a property of\nthe current bit layout rather than of the threading: osd-zfs does have\ncross-thread writers to this word, os_paused and os_waiting, which\nmerely land in the other byte.  Lock it too so the rule holds by\nconstruction.\n\nTest-Parameters: trivial\nFixes: 0c1ae1cb9c19 (\"LU-13124 scrub: check for multiple linked file\")\nSigned-off-by: Oleg Drokin <green@whamcloud.com>\nAssisted-by: ClaudeCode:Opus-5 llm_code_and_review_tools\nChange-Id: I7497b7d2726656189cc1c8b24f4d14aedc75fed8\n"
      },
      "branch": "refs/heads/master"
    }
